title: Deleting a metarecord in the store
tags: Storage
kind: rationale
summary: A deletion removes a metarecord's rows and takes its id out of the universe, and leaves what the indexes hold about it until a reindex: on a hard disk each index entry is a seek, so every answer is sifted through the universe instead.
audience: dev


Deleting a metarecord used to unpick everything the store held about it: each row's
entries in the value partitions, the trigrams of its texts, its bitmaps, its owner
and field entries. On a hard disk that is the whole cost of a deletion — not the
volume, a few kilobytes, but the ''number of places'': each key lives on a page
somewhere else, and each page is a seek (about 9 ms to read cold, 3 ms to write,
measured on a USB hard disk in November 2026). One file of eight rows wrote 69 keys
outside the log, and deleting one file took 760 ms on that disk with the store held
in memory.

!! What a deletion writes

|!Written |!Why |
|its record (`records`, one key) and its existence (`metarecords`) |the primary data |
|its id out of the ''universe'' set |what makes it gone from every answer |
|`field_types`, the histograms |they count rows, and no reader can sift a count; both are small tables |
|its parent out of the `parents` set, if it was the last child |a fact about ''another'' metarecord |
|the operation and its snapshots |the log, appended ([[The operation log]]) |

Everything else stays: the value and name partitions, the trigrams, the
postings, the `present` / `absent` / `multi` sets, the referrers and descendants, the
`kids`, the id's mapping to its uuid — and, in the primary tables, its key in
`forest`. The same file of eight rows now writes its record's key and its chunk of
the universe outside the log and those small tables.

!! Why what is left is harmless

* ''A dense id is never given again.'' What still names a deleted id names nothing; a metarecord created again under the same uuid (a revert of the deletion) gets a new id, so the old entries never come back to life — and what points at that uuid, filed under the former id, is moved under the new one ([[Store tables]]).
* ''Every answer is sifted through the universe.'' The query source intersects each bitmap it hands the evaluator with the universe, read once per query (`KvSource::sift`); a walk of a partition steps over the ids that are not in it. An entry is about one id, so a deleted id's entries cannot change what is said of another.
* ''The primary leftover names a record that is gone.'' A `forest` entry holds its metarecord's dense id beside its uuid, so listing a folder or resolving a path tests the id against the universe without another read; a new position written on such a key replaces it.

What the universe cannot sift is kept exact at the deletion: the counts above, and
the `parents` set. One case is not taken the short way: a metarecord that still has
children that exist — the `Writer` refuses to commit that, but a write may delete a
folder before its files — is unpicked row by row as before, because its subtree must
leave its ancestors' descendants ([[The forest in the store]]).

!! What it costs

* Each query reads the universe (one key per 65 536 metarecords) and intersects its leaves with it.
* The leftovers take room, and slow the scans that cross them, until `mf repo reindex` drops them: it rebuilds the indexes from the rows, removes the primary leftovers, and writes the new ids into `forest` ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]). So [[Compacting a repository]] gives back the room of a large deletion only after a reindex.
* `mf repo check` holds the indexes to the rows ''on the ids of the metarecords that exist''; an id that was never given is still reported.

The tests are `tests/kv_delete.rs`: which tables a deletion writes
(`KvStore::last_write_keys`), and every kind of answer held to the oracle after
deletions, reverts and a reindex.

!! Deleting many records

A deletion of many records at once does less still: the records stay in their keys and
the key of each uuid in `metarecords`, until an idle fold, and only the universe, the type
counts and the parents change ([[Mass operations in the log]]).
